<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Marshall Islands Protectorate</h1><p class="st-meta">1885 CE – 1906 CE · capital Jaluit</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A German protectorate over the Marshall Islands and later Nauru, run largely by a Hamburg trading company for the copra trade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>German traders dominated copra buying in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-islands">Marshall Islands</a> from the 1870s, and in 1878 a German naval officer secured a treaty with chiefs on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaluit-atoll">Jaluit</a>. In 1885, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> were dividing the western Pacific into spheres of influence, Germany declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, with an imperial commissioner at Jaluit.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From 1888 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaluit-gesellschaft">Jaluit Gesellschaft</a>, a merger of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Hamburg</a> trading houses, paid the costs of administration in return for a trade monopoly, so the colony was in effect run by a company. Marshallese paramount chiefs, the iroij, kept authority over land and people and collected copra as tribute. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/territory-of-nauru">Nauru</a> was annexed in 1888 and attached to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. Protestant missionaries from the American Board were active.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Reich took over administration from the company, and in 1906 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> was merged into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/german-new-guinea">German New Guinea</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied the islands in 1914, ruling them under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/south-seas-mandate">South Seas Mandate</a>, while <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/territory-of-nauru">Nauru</a> became the Territory of Nauru.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><di